-      <h2>6. The conjugate root theorem</h2>
+      <h2>6. Using De Moivre's theorem to prove trig identities</h2>
+      <p>De Moivre's theorem isn't only a picture of rotation &mdash; expand \((\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^n\) with the Binomial Theorem and compare it to \(\cos n\theta+i\sin n\theta\), and the real and imaginary parts hand you formulas for \(\cos n\theta\) and \(\sin n\theta\) purely in terms of \(\cos\theta\) and \(\sin\theta\). This is a standard exam technique in its own right, and it's really the same rotation picture from section 5, just read off in coordinates instead of drawn.</p>
+
+      <div class="demo">
+        <span class="demo-label">Worked algebra &mdash; cos 3&theta; and sin 3&theta; via De Moivre's theorem</span>
+        <p>By De Moivre's theorem with \(n=3\):</p>
+        <p>\[(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^3 = \cos3\theta+i\sin3\theta\]</p>
+        <p>Expand the left-hand side with the Binomial Theorem:</p>
+        <p>\[(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)^3 = \cos^3\theta+3\cos^2\theta(i\sin\theta)+3\cos\theta(i\sin\theta)^2+(i\sin\theta)^3\]</p>
+        <p>\[=(\cos^3\theta-3\cos\theta\sin^2\theta)+i(3\cos^2\theta\sin\theta-\sin^3\theta)\]</p>
+        <p>Equating this to \(\cos3\theta+i\sin3\theta\) and matching real parts with real, imaginary with imaginary:</p>
+        <p>\[\cos3\theta=\cos^3\theta-3\cos\theta\sin^2\theta, \qquad \sin3\theta=3\cos^2\theta\sin\theta-\sin^3\theta\]</p>
+        <p>Substitute \(\sin^2\theta=1-\cos^2\theta\) into the first, and \(\cos^2\theta=1-\sin^2\theta\) into the second, then simplify:</p>
+        <p>\[\cos3\theta=\cos^3\theta-3\cos\theta(1-\cos^2\theta)=4\cos^3\theta-3\cos\theta\]</p>
+        <p>\[\sin3\theta=3(1-\sin^2\theta)\sin\theta-\sin^3\theta=3\sin\theta-4\sin^3\theta\]</p>
+        <p>Same method, any \(n\): expand, split into real and imaginary parts, simplify. The picture (section 5) tells you <em>why</em> this works; the algebra is what actually gets you the identity on the page.</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <h2>7. The conjugate root theorem</h2>
       <p>The conjugate of \(z=a+bi\) is \(\bar z = a-bi\) &mdash; geometrically, \(z\) reflected across the real axis. Suppose \(p(z)\) is a polynomial with <em>real</em> coefficients and \(p(z)=0\). Taking the conjugate of both sides, every real coefficient survives untouched (conjugating a real number does nothing) &mdash; only \(z\) itself gets reflected &mdash; giving \(p(\bar z)=\overline{p(z)}=\bar 0=0\). So \(\bar z\) is a root too: for a real polynomial, non-real roots always come in mirror-image pairs across the real axis.</p>
 
